Clod's specimen (SEQ 697, 2026-11-18), above the stop:
    rsMid reads a cell up to the set held in rsDelim, then the comma as its second term; rsLast reads a cell up to the
    set, then the newline. rsRead tries rsMid, then rsLast, until neither matches: two rows of three cells.
    The ^ on the second term matters: without it the skipper eats the newline before the term sees it, and the first
    row runs on into the second.
    The data ends in a line with no delimiter after it on purpose. A rule that reads a pushed field to its very end
    loses the rest of the calling file at exit 0 (string delimiters too, not only sets; in the fixit upToSetDelimiter).

What this means for DataInputField above (left as you wrote it):
    1. Its second term wants the ^ (dataDelimiter^), for the newline, as rsMid and rsLast have.
    2. Its body's test `if dataDelimiter != ","` reads unequal for every field: a term's label compared with ==
       or != never reads equal, however spelled (dataDelimiter == ",", .text, a := capture, a field holding ","),
       although it prints right. Measured SEQ 697; in the fixit upToSetDelimiter. rsMid / rsLast tell the comma
       from the newline by which rule matched instead.
    3. guard='n' is still needed (item 2 of the fixit, Tony's ensureGuard note: not built).
